{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And YHWH Elohim made for the man and his wife coats of skin.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He made them fine and distinguished garments of skin, in which they would wrap their entire body in a manner of honor, for it would have been a disgrace to their dignity if they had stood in those girdles they had made for themselves, which covered only the flesh of nakedness. And perhaps this skin was the skin of the תחש (tachash) that existed in the days of Moses, created for its hour for their honor, that they might cover themselves with it; and it was like that tachash skin that existed in the days of Moses, created for its hour to cover the Ark and its vessels, and hidden away thereafter — and it had colors and many kinds of hues, as Onkelos translated ססגונא — which rejoiced in many hues. And the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory is that these garments were painted there with every kind of bird in the world, and Adam bequeathed them to Cain, and when Cain was killed they came into the hands of Nimrod — that is what is written: ״הוא היה גבור ציד״ — 'he was a mighty hunter.' And when Esau killed Nimrod, Esau took them from him, and that is what is written (Genesis 27:15): ״את בגדי עשו בנה הגדול החמודות״ — 'the garments of Esau your firstborn son, the precious ones' — what is ״החמודות״ (the precious ones)? That he coveted them from Nimrod." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: כתנות עור — in the Torah scroll of Rabbi Meir it was written כתנות אור — 'coats of light.' The intention is that the Holy One, blessed be He, clothed them with kinds of excellencies and illuminations from the supernal light that were in the Garden of Eden, like Moses who merited them on the mountain. And from the greatness of the Clother you may infer the greatness of the garment, for the intention in them was eternal life, and that they would be like the ministering angels." "<b>And He clothed them.</b> It should have said כתנות עור ללבוש — 'coats of skin to wear' — in the manner of what is written (Genesis 28:20): ״ובגד ללבוש״ — 'and clothing to wear.' But by way of the plain sense (peshat), He wished to ascribe the act of clothing to Him, blessed be He, to indicate His love and compassion for His creatures: even though they sinned, He did not cease cherishing them, and He Himself attended to their rectification and the bestowal of kindnesses. And so we find that He conducted Himself thus with the children in the wilderness: He ascribed the act of bringing down the manna to Him, blessed be He, in the manner of what is written: ״הנני ממטיר לכם לחם מן השמים״ — even though they sinned with the likeness of the sin of Adam, He did not cease cherishing them, nor did He withdraw His kindness from them, nor did the manna cease to fall as at the beginning. And this is what Ezra said (Nehemiah 9:20): ״ומנך לא מנעת מפיהם״ — 'and You did not withhold Your manna from their mouth.' From here, that they were worthy of having it withheld from them. And so we find in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds: even though idolatry was among them, He did not refrain from splitting the sea, as is written (Zechariah 10:11): ״ועבר בים צרה״ — 'and He passed through the sea of distress' — this is the idol of Micah. ״צרה״ — 'distress' — is idolatry, as is written (Isaiah 28:20): ״והמסכה צרה כהתכנס״. Behold, all this is the kindness of HaShem, may He be exalted, and concerning this Scripture says (Daniel 9:7): ״לך ה' הצדקה ולנו בושת הפנים״ — 'to You, O YHWH, belongs righteousness, and to us shame of face.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: וילבישם — 'And He clothed them' — in the garments of the high priesthood He clothed them. It is written here ״וילבישם״, and it is written there ״וילבישם כתנות״ (Leviticus 8:13). And it is known that the garments of the high priesthood were eight, and likewise in the verse ״ויעש ה' אלהים לאדם ולאשתו כתנות עור וילבישם״ — there are eight words, and understand this." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): In this section there is a great allusion to the Torah and to the commandments, for whose sake the creation of man was fundamentally undertaken, and it contains an allusion to the body and to the soul and to the evil impulse (yetzer ha-ra). And it contains an allusion to the resurrection of the dead and to the Day of Judgment, and how man is called to account over the Torah and over the commandments. And it contains an allusion to reward and to punishment, for the body and for the soul. It also contains yet another allusion to the uprooting of the evil impulse (yetzer ha-ra) from the world. And now I shall explain for you the matter of the section in general terms, and you will understand the particulars from within the general." "<b>And HaShem Elohim took the human and placed him in the Garden of Eden to work it and to guard it.</b> The Garden of Eden is an allusion to the Torah; and just as the garden is drawn from Eden, so the Torah is drawn from the supernal wisdom, which is Eden. And the essential purpose of the human's creation is that he be engaged in the business of Torah — 'to work it and to guard it.'" "<b>And HaShem Elohim commanded the human.</b> It is known that the essence of the Torah consists of positive commandments and negative commandments. If so, the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge, which are fixed in the midst of the garden, allude to the positive commandments and negative commandments fixed in the midst of the Torah. And the human alludes to the intellect, and the intellect is not good being alone, for it needs a vessel in which it may display its operations and through which it may have help in fulfilling the Torah and the commandments; and this vessel is matter. And since the human was created with body and soul, he needed help in the perpetuation of the species, just as the body helps the intellect in matters of the intellect — and this help is the woman, and she contains an allusion to the body. Thus Solomon too likened matter to a woman, as he said (Proverbs 2:16): להצילך מאשה זרה — 'to deliver you from a strange woman,' and so forth — because the woman receives action, just as matter receives forms and bears the configurations. And the serpent contains an allusion to the evil inclination, from the root מנחש, for it tests the human and entices him. And it is the one that begins speaking with the woman — that is, with matter — and the beginning of the words of its mouth is אף, for anger and wrath are drawn from its power. And the explanation of its words: אף — though Elohim said, 'you shall not eat of every tree of the garden' — if the Holy One, blessed be He, withheld from you the permission to eat of all the body's desires, did He not withhold from you all desires and all the good things in the world? And how is it possible that He should command you such a thing? — this is the enticement of the evil inclination. And the reason we find it speaking with the woman and not with the human is that all the effort of the evil inclination is directed only at matter, not at the intellectual faculty." "<b>And the woman said to the serpent: of the fruit of the garden we may eat.</b> The reply of matter. He did not withhold from it all bodily desire, for it was for eating and drinking that He created us, to sustain the life of the body; but He warned us concerning some of them — that we should not use them and derive benefit from them except for the perpetuation of the species. And should it be that we transgress His commandment, the soul would die together with the body." "<b>And the serpent said to the woman.</b> The words of the evil inclination to matter. לא מות תמותון — 'you shall not surely die' — here it added in its enticement and said: it is impossible that this should be so, for on account of bodily pleasures you shall not die, for a human being cannot live without them. כי יודע אלהים כי ביום אכלכם ממנו ונפקחו עיניכם — 'for Elohim knows that on the day you eat of it your eyes will be opened.' That is: as long as you engage in pleasures and are drawn after the body's desires, you will understand more and you will have the power of choice and will between good and evil. And now it added further and enticed more and more, saying: try only this once and begin to engage in pleasures, and then you will recognize the benefit, for your eyes will be opened and you will know and distinguish between good and evil, and you will abandon the evil and choose the good. And the serpent's intention was that once they knew that this was good and that was evil, they would spread out even into evil." "<b>And the woman saw.</b> This is the seeing of the heart. That is: matter gazed at bodily matters and inclined after its heart. כי טוב העץ למאכל — 'that the tree was good for food' — that it is good to engage in bodily desires. And the word מאכל includes also sexual intercourse, from the language of (Proverbs 30:20) אכלה ומחתה פיה — 'she ate and wiped her mouth.' וכי תאוה הוא לעינים — 'and that it was desirable to the eyes' — for the heart and the eyes are the agents of desire, as it is written (Numbers 15:39): ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם — 'and you shall not go astray after your hearts and after your eyes.' ותקח מפריו ותאכל — 'and she took of its fruit and ate' — matter engaged in bodily desires until it drew in the intellectual faculty; this is ותתן גם לאישה עמה — 'and she gave also to her husband with her.' For just as the primordial serpent deceived Havah, and Havah deceived the human, according to the plain sense of the matter — for Scripture does not depart from its plain sense — so too the evil inclination deceives matter and entices it with the pleasures of the world, and matter inclines the intellectual faculty toward itself and draws it to its side." "<b>And the eyes of them both were opened.</b> It is the way of matter and the intellective faculty — that is, the body and the soul — that after they sin they become aware of their sin and recognize their deficiency, how they sinned and how they clothed themselves in desires. This is what it says: ״ותפקחנה עיני שניהם וידעו״. And this opening of the eyes is not in wisdom, nor in the seeing of the eye, nor in the attainment of any possession, but rather in the absence of a possession: for their eyes were opened and they recognized the absence of their attainment, for at first they were intellectual beings who knew nothing of desire at all, and now they clothed themselves in it. And this is what it says: ״וידעו״ — and the meaning of this word is that they drew the force of desire from the Tree of Knowledge. And this is ״ותפקחנה עיני שניהם״ — that they now perceived the absence of the excellent possession they had acquired at first, which was the intellect, and now it had departed from them, like a rich man whose wealth is lost and whose eyes are now opened by the absence of his wealth, and he now recognizes the great worth of what he had at first — for a man does not recognize the value of a thing while it is in his hand until it is lost from him. ״כי ערומים הם״ — and by the force of the desires in which they clothed themselves, the matter became shameful, which they had not considered shameful at first. So too the body and the soul find themselves naked of intellect and of merit after their sin, and then they turn to their heart and reflect upon the warnings written in the Torah from the mouth of the Almighty, and they are ashamed and humiliated like the shame of a thief when caught for not having fulfilled them as they were commanded in the Torah, which is likened to a garden. This is what it says: ״וישמעו את קול יהוה מתהלך בגן לרוח היום ויתחבא האדם ואשתו מפני יהוה אלהים״. And behold, the man — that is, the intellective faculty — is called to account here on the day of judgment, when all the creatures of the world are summoned before the King. This is what it says: ״ויקרא יהוה אלהים אל האדם ויאמר לו איכה״ — in what place are you, that is, in what degree have you ascended in the fulfillment of Torah and commandments. And the addition of the letter ה in איכה alludes to the five books of the Torah, about which one is called to account on the day of judgment, for it could have been written איך without a ה, as in (Psalms 139:8) ״ואציעה שאול הנך״." "<b>And he said, Your voice I heard in the garden.</b> The intellective faculty answered: I understood your intent and I heard your warnings written in the Torah, and I fear the day of judgment. ״כי ערום אנכי״ — for I am naked of Torah and of merit — and therefore it said אנכי and not אני, ״ואחבא״ — and I hide, for I am ashamed, for the way of one who is ashamed of something is to hide. ״מי הגיד לך״ — who told you? Who was the cause that you are naked of merit? ״המן העץ אשר צויתיך״ — was it from the tree concerning which I commanded you? Did you transgress my commandment and do what I forbade you?" "<b>And the man said, The woman whom you gave with me.</b> The intellective faculty answered: I transgressed your commandment, and the cause is matter — that is, the matter which you gave with me to be a help to me — it frustrated your intent and it caused me this great distress, and this is the meaning of ״היא״. And the addition of the ה in the word נתתה corresponds to the five senses that are drawn after the matter within them, satisfying their desires; he comes to blame the matter. And he answered: ״הנחש השיאני״ — that is, the evil inclination deceived me, so that I transgressed your commandment, for matter, were it not for the evil inclination, would know nothing of evil — he comes to blame the evil inclination. And He cursed it: ״על גחונך תלך״ — and because until now it ruled over all creatures, and this is what King Solomon, peace be upon him, called ״מלך גדול״, and the way of a king is to walk with an upright posture, therefore He cursed it that He would humble its pride and nullify its power." "<b>And dust shall you eat all the days of your life.</b> All the days of the resurrection of the dead, dust shall you eat — not the flesh of creatures. And the meaning is: if you can cause the dust to sin, for over the creatures you have no authority. ״ואיבה אשית״ — the meaning of this enmity is that the evil inclination will be distanced at that time, for one who has enmity with another distances himself from him. And the intent is that the Holy One, blessed be He, will distance in those days between the evil inclination and matter, for in the era of the resurrection of the dead their matter will be pure and clean, submissive to the soul — no adversary and no evil occurrence, and this is ״ואיבה אשית בינך ובין האשה״ — a hint that through this distance the evil inclination will be uprooted from the world and its power nullified, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 25:8) ״בלע המות לנצח״. He comes to punish matter. This is ״ולאשה אמר הרבה ארבה״ — that is, since you transgressed my commandment and did not guard yourself against the seduction of the evil inclination, I will punish you; for just as you grew accustomed to bodily desires and multiplied rejoicing in the vanities of the world, so I will multiply for you punishments and afflictions, reproaches and everlasting abhorrence. And this is the doubling of ״הרבה ארבה״ — for as much as you multiplied rejoicing and pleasure, I will multiply for you the punishment. He comes to punish the intellective faculty. This is ״ולאדם אמר כי שמעת לקול אשתך״ — that is, you were drawn after matter and transgressed my commandment. ״ארורה האדמה בעבורך״ — He added a curse upon the matter in which the intellective faculty manifests its operations. And He called matter by the term אדמה, and said ״בעצבון תאכלנה״ — He punished it that matter shall not be drawn according to its will all the days of the resurrection of the dead, and behold this is a forceful curse upon the intellective faculty. And Daniel, peace be upon him, already taught us this matter, that in the era of the resurrection of the dead their condition will not be equal, for these shall arise for a reward and recompense unlike anything in all the ages, and those shall arise for a great punishment that has no end — this is what he said (Daniel 12:2) ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם״. And He said ״כי עפר אתה ואל עפר תשוב״ — for since the intellective faculty was drawn after matter, its end is to perish with the perishing of matter. And the reason this passage is sealed with the verse ״כתנות עור וילבישם״ is because it speaks of that class which is for eternal life." "<b>And there is</b> for you to observe how He punished the first ones who were of that class which is ״לחרפות לדראון עולם״, and since they did not merit eternal life and listened to the voice of matter, He punished their body and their soul, and in that punishment He did not mention His name, blessed be He. But in this verse, regarding the class which is for eternal life, He mentions His name, blessed be He, and says ״ויעש יהוה אלהים״. And this verse uses the past in place of the future, in the manner of prophecies. And it appears to me to interpret the word ויעש in this place as a new creation (בריה חדשה), and אדם ואשתו in this verse refers to the intellect and the soul, for it is possible to liken the soul to a female and the intellect to a male, because the soul cleaves to the intellect just as the female cleaves to the male; and so my master and teacher, may his memory be for a blessing, interpreted in the matter of Leviathan and its mate, that it contains an allusion to the intellect and the soul. And this verse alludes to the era of the resurrection of the dead, and it says that at that time the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make for the intellect and the soul a new creation, restoring to them ״כתנות עור״ — that is, the bodies — and this is what it says: ״וילבישם״. And thus Scripture mentions regarding the formation of man (Job 10:11) ״עור ובשר תלבישני״ — so too here the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to clothe the bodies for the intellect and the soul, and then there will be a new creation that the Holy One, blessed be He, will renew His world at that time, in the manner of what the prophet said (Isaiah 66:22) ״והארץ החדשה אשר אני עושה״. And I found support for myself from what they said in the Midrash (Genesis 12:2) on ״ואעשך לגוי גדול״: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, behold I am creating for you a new creation, as it says ״ויעש אלהים את הרקיע״. And further, כתנות עור may include כתנות אור as the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted it, for alef and ayin are interchangeable. And the meaning is that the Holy One, blessed be He, will clothe the soul and the body together with sorts of supernal light's excellencies, as Moses, peace be upon him, merited on the mountain, from the verse (Exodus 33:7) ״ומשה יקח את האהל״, which the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted from the language of (Job 29:3) ״בהלו נרו״. And this is according to the first interpretation we gave, that אדם ואשתו contains an allusion to the soul and the body throughout the entire passage, and on this it says ״וילבישם״ and did not say כתנות עור ללבוש. For He, blessed be He, is the one who clothes them, and from the excellence of the Clother you can understand the excellence of the garment. And understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:flags {10 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {2 "The phrase ועבר בים צרה is cited from Zechariah 10:11; the citation in the printed Hebrew reads (זכריה י׳:י״א), normalized. The prooftext from Isaiah 28:20 (והמסכה צרה כהתכנס) is obscure and rendered hyper-literally; it may be textually corrupt.", 14 "The lemma ויש is incomplete; the comment opens with ויש לך להסתכל, rendered as 'And there is for you to observe.' The Midrash citation 'Genesis 12:2' refers to a passage in Bereshit Rabbah on Genesis 12:2, not to the biblical verse itself."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:12.591873659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}